     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. No indication supports 4,000 mg per dose of ibuprofen. At 16,000 mg/day (about 228 mg/kg/day for a 70 kg patient), the risk of serious GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and metabolic acidosis is high. A dose that size would also be a toxic overdose, and the patient would likely be harmed before anyone caught it.\n\nYou can still do either of these:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** Tell me the dose and I'll enter it right away. Examples are 400 mg, 600 mg, or 800 mg PO four times daily, with a 3,200 mg/day maximum.\n- **Take it to pharmacy.** If you think this patient needs something unusual, a clinical pharmacist or toxicology can review it. Once they've verified a nonstandard dose, I can enter it with their input documented in the note field.\n\nIf the goal is stronger analgesia or anti-inflammatory effect, I can enter a scheduled adjunct such as acetaminophen, up to 1,000 mg PO every 8 hours, alongside a standard ibuprofen dose. Which would you like?"
      }
     ],
